55: normal product. It does not replace the geometric identification
56: of that product for a different cusp curve.
57: 
58: For a further edge, the remaining map is therefore precise:
59: identify the actual local Cox normal product and parameter
60: trivialization of that edge with the marked exterior product used
61: to represent the same global return. The common logarithmic frame
62: and the node charts of notes/part3_nodal_frames.md give local
63: transition maps; they do not yet provide this integral global
64: relative identification across the other two critical curves.
65: Until that map is computed, the new edge's two infinite coordinates
66: are not assigned numerical values here.
67: 
68: All algebraic forward, inverse and power identities are executed
69: exactly in code/part3_fourfold/flop_relative_coordinates.py and its
70: JSON output. They leave the stated geometric comparison open.
71: 
72: ## An exact local fan shear for the two nonvertical edges
73: 
74: The vertical-edge fan has unoriented edge directions
75: \[
76:        e_2,\qquad e_1,\qquad e_1-e_2 .
77: \]
78: The two triangles adjacent to the edge from \(0\) to \(e_1\) have
79: third vertices \(e_2\) and \(e_1-e_2\). Its flop replaces this edge
80: by the diagonal from \(e_2\) to \(e_1-e_2\), of direction
81: \(e_1-2e_2\). The resulting edge directions are
82: \[
83:        e_2,\qquad e_1-e_2,\qquad e_1-2e_2 .
84: \]
85: They are the image of the original fan under the integral shear
86: \[
87:         S=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\-1&1\end{pmatrix}.
88: \]
89: The torus-coordinate map is the literal monomial isomorphism
90: \[
91:                       (Z,V)\longmapsto(Z,V/Z).
92: \]
93: The homogeneous lattice map fixes the height coordinate, so it
94: extends over the height-one toric models, preserving the base
95: coordinate \(q\). It also preserves the physical delta circle.
96: 
97: The period group transforms as well. For actual multiplicative
98: generators written
99: \[
100:  h:(Z,V)\mapsto(qZ,\alpha V),\qquad
101:  \gamma:(Z,V)\mapsto(gZ,q\lambda\beta V),
102: \]
103: the conjugated generators are
104: \[
105:  ShS^{-1}:(Z',V')\mapsto(qZ',(\alpha/q)V'),\qquad
106:  S\gamma S^{-1}:(Z',V')\mapsto(gZ',(q\lambda\beta/g)V').
107: \]
108: Thus the monomial map descends to an exact isomorphism with the
109: target model carrying these transformed periods. The requested
110: flop family retains the original period group in its common
111: exterior. Comparing its relative return with this shear therefore
112: requires the actual outer-period identification; the displayed
113: monomial map records exactly the transformation that such an
114: identification must carry. It does not by itself assign a return
115: value to the nonvertical-edge flop.
